\name{Ggplot2SurvivalLayerProcessor}
\alias{Ggplot2SurvivalLayerProcessor}
\title{ggplot2 Kaplan-Meier Layer Processor}
\description{
Reads a \code{\link[=maidr_survival]{maidr_survival()}} layer as the \code{survival} trace: one series per
stratum, each point a row of the layer, with \code{censored} where a subject
left the study at that time and the band's \code{yMin} and \code{yMax} when the
layer draws it.

A Kaplan-Meier curve is a step chart, so the step processor does the
work -- the rows in drawn order, the series split, the x and y read back
through the scales, \code{stepDirection} (always \code{"hv"}). What this adds is
what the trace reads that a step does not, and where the curve is.
\itemize{
\item \strong{Censoring and the band travel with the points.} \code{GeomSurvival}
names \code{censored}, \code{ymin} and \code{ymax} as aesthetics, so they survive the
build as columns. The rows the step processor emitted points for are
found again by the same panel filter, drawn order, group split and
\code{NA} span, and each value is put beside its point; a series whose count
disagrees gets nothing rather than another row's.
\item \strong{The curve is found in the layer's own tree.} \code{GeomSurvival} draws
its band, curve and marks as one grob tree named after the geom, the
curve a bare polyline with one line per stratum. The tree is found by
the layer's slot in the panel and the polyline inside it, so no other
layer's polyline can be taken for it, nor it for theirs.
}
}
